"We have the system of equations\n\n\\begin{align*}\np+q&=pq\\\\\np-q-q&=7.\n\\end{align*}\n\nFrom the second equation, we have $p-q=7$. Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$p+q=7p\\Rightarrow p=1.$$\n\nTherefore, $q=8$. Now, we need to find $\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{p^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}$. We have\n\n$$\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{p^2}+\\frac{1}{q^2}}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{1}{1}+\\frac{1}{64}}=\\frac{1}{\\frac{65}{64}}=\\frac{64}{65}.$$\n\nTherefore, the answer is $\\frac{64}{65}$, which is of the form $\\frac{a+b\\sqrt{c}}{d}$ with $a=1$, $b=0$, $c=1$, and $d=65$. Thus, $a+b+c+d=1+0+1+65=\\boxed{67}$."
