"We can rewrite the equation as\n\n$$(2^x-2)(5\\cdot2^x+10)=3\\cdot4^x+12.$$\n\nSince $4^x=(2^{2x})^x$, we have\n\n$$(2^x-2)(5\\cdot2^x+10)=3\\cdot2^{2x}+12.$$\n\nExpanding and simplifying, we get\n\n$$10\\cdot2^x-20=3\\cdot2^{2x}+12.$$\n\nRearranging terms, we have\n\n$$10\\cdot2^x-3\\cdot2^{2x}=12+20.$$\n\nSimplifying, we get\n\n$$10\\cdot2^x-3\\cdot2^{2x}=32.$$\n\nFactoring out $2^x$, we have\n\n$$2^x(10-3\\cdot2^x)=32.$$\n\nSince $2^x$ is nonzero, we can divide both sides by $2^x$:\n\n$$10-3\\cdot2^x=4.$$\n\nSolving for $x$, we get:\n\n$$x=\\boxed{2}.$$"
