"First, we need to determine the total number of ways to choose 3 people out of 10. This can be calculated using the combination formula:\n\n$$\\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 120.$$\n\nNext, we need to determine the number of ways to choose 3 people sitting in consecutive seats. Since the table is round, there are 10 possible of consecutive people (one for each starting position). Therefore, there are 10 ways to choose 3 people sitting in consecutive seats.\n\nThe probability that the three chosen people were sitting in consecutive seats is the ratio of the number of favorable outcomes to the total number of outcomes:\n\n$$\\frac{10}{120} = \\frac{1}{12}.$$\n\nThus, the probability that the three chosen people were sitting in consecutive seats is $\\boxed{\\frac{1}{12}}$."
